## Renaming

`name` is **not** a replace-forcing attribute. The v3 API has a dedicated rename route, so changing `name` renames the namespace in place: it keeps its id and its orbs, and Terraform reports a plain update rather than a destroy and create.

A rename is still disruptive outside Terraform's view of the world:

* orbs in the namespace are reachable only under the new name, so any `.circleci/config.yml` that references `<old-namespace>/<orb>@<version>` stops resolving;
* runner resource classes named `<old-namespace>/<class>` stop matching.

The provider emits a warning after a successful rename to say so.

`organization_id` cannot change: a namespace cannot be moved between organizations, so changing it forces a new namespace, which deletes the old one and everything in it.

{{ .SchemaMarkdown | trimspace }}

## Import

The import ID is `<organization_id>/<namespace_name>`, or `<organization_id>/<namespace_id>`.

{{ codefile "shell" "examples/resources/circleci_orb_namespace/import.sh" }}

The organization id has to be part of the import ID because the CircleCI API's namespace representation does not include the owning organization, so the read that follows the import cannot discover it. Importing with the name alone would leave `organization_id` null, and the next plan would then want to replace the namespace — destroying every orb in it. The provider therefore rejects a bare name with an explicit error.
